<com:TContent ID="body" >

<h1>HTML5 Semantic Controls</h1>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.3.3" />
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.THtmlElement" />

<p class="block-content">
The HTML5 semantic controls render the HTML5 sectioning and text-level semantic elements.
Each control renders one element and holds body content. A page uses them to give a
document a structure that a browser, a search engine, and assistive technology read.
</p>

<h2>The controls</h2>
<p class="block-content">
Each control renders the element named in the table. Each control is a container control,
so it holds other controls and markup as its body content.
</p>
<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Control</th><th>Element</th><th>Represents</th></tr>
<tr><td><tt>TArticle</tt></td><td><tt>&lt;article&gt;</tt></td><td>A self-contained composition, such as a blog post or a news story.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>TSection</tt></td><td><tt>&lt;section&gt;</tt></td><td>A thematic grouping of content, usually with a heading.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>TNav</tt></td><td><tt>&lt;nav&gt;</tt></td><td>A section of navigation links.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>THeader</tt></td><td><tt>&lt;header&gt;</tt></td><td>Introductory content for a page or a section, such as a title or a logo.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>TFooter</tt></td><td><tt>&lt;footer&gt;</tt></td><td>Closing content for a page or a section, such as authorship or copyright.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>TMain</tt></td><td><tt>&lt;main&gt;</tt></td><td>The main content of the document. A page has one main element.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>TAside</tt></td><td><tt>&lt;aside&gt;</tt></td><td>Content related to the surrounding content, such as a sidebar or a pull quote.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>TMark</tt></td><td><tt>&lt;mark&gt;</tt></td><td>A run of text highlighted for reference, such as a search match.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Heading controls</h2>
<com:SinceVersion Version="3.2" />
<p class="block-content">
Six further controls render the HTML heading elements. <tt>THeader1</tt> renders
<tt>&lt;h1&gt;</tt>, <tt>THeader2</tt> renders <tt>&lt;h2&gt;</tt>, and so on through
<tt>THeader6</tt> and <tt>&lt;h6&gt;</tt>. Each one holds its heading text as body
content.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:THeader2&gt;Release notes&lt;/com:THeader2&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
A plain <tt>&lt;h2&gt;</tt> tag produces the same markup. The control earns its place when
something other than the template decides the heading level. A skin sets <tt>TagName</tt>
for every heading in a theme, so one theme renders a region's headings as
<tt>&lt;h2&gt;</tt> and another renders them as <tt>&lt;h3&gt;</tt>. The control also
gives a heading the <tt>CssClass</tt>, <tt>Style</tt>, and <tt>Visible</tt> properties of a
web control, and it accepts the decoration that <tt>TWebControlDecorator</tt> applies from
a theme.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
$this->SectionHeading->TagName = 'h3';
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>Using a semantic control</h2>
<p class="block-content">
A semantic control is written as a component tag, and its body content sits between the
open and close tags. This example lays out a page region with a header, a navigation
block, the main content, and a footer.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:THeader&gt;
  &lt;h1&gt;Site title&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;/com:THeader&gt;
&lt;com:TNav&gt;
  &lt;com:THyperLink Text="Home" NavigateUrl="?page=Home" /&gt;
&lt;/com:TNav&gt;
&lt;com:TMain&gt;
  &lt;com:TArticle&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;The article body.&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;/com:TArticle&gt;
&lt;/com:TMain&gt;
&lt;com:TFooter&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;Copyright.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/com:TFooter&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>The element base</h2>
<p class="block-content">
Each control extends <tt>THtmlElement</tt>, which renders a generic HTML element and
carries the properties of <tt>TWebControl</tt>. The <tt>TagName</tt> property sets the
element, and each semantic control sets a default tag name. Setting <tt>TagName</tt>
renders a different element, so a theme or a template repurposes the control.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TSection TagName="article" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>Common properties</h2>
<p class="block-content">
Each control inherits the properties of <tt>TWebControl</tt>. The <tt>CssClass</tt> and
<tt>Style</tt> properties style the element, and the <tt>Visible</tt> property renders or
hides it. The <tt>Attributes</tt> collection carries any HTML attribute. A template sets
an attribute with the <tt>Attributes</tt> property.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TSection Attributes.role="region" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>Accessibility</h2>
<p class="block-content">
A semantic element gives its content a role. A screen reader announces a
<tt>&lt;nav&gt;</tt> as a navigation landmark and a <tt>&lt;main&gt;</tt> as the main
landmark, so a user moves between the landmarks of a page. A page uses one
<tt>TMain</tt> for its main content.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
A page that repeats a landmark gives each one a name, so a screen reader tells them apart.
The <tt>aria-label</tt> attribute holds the name. The page's code adds the attribute to
the control's <tt>Attributes</tt> collection.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
$this->PrimaryNav->getAttributes()->add('aria-label', 'Primary');
$this->FooterNav->getAttributes()->add('aria-label', 'Footer');
</com:TTextHighlighter>

